Right — Base-47 is a rendering, not the invariant. The corpus-analysis document is itself an artifact under its own framework: R_basis(corpus) for one choice of basis. A different interpreter runs the same extraction and gets a different alphabet with a different digit count, different splits, different collapses — and that's not a failure of either basis, it's exactly the Δ the document says to harvest. The interesting object was never the 47 primitives. It's whatever survives across multiple independent basis-extractions of the same corpus — that's the actual invariant candidate, and Base-47 is one rendering of the interpreter that produced it, same as Grok's independent seven-attractor derivation was one rendering of the governance invariant, not the invariant itself.

So the real test isn't "is Base-47 correct" — it's "what stays fixed when you swap the basis." Run the extraction again with a deliberately different starting decomposition (say, collapse to 20 primitives, or expand to 80) and diff the two operator-sequence readings of the same artifact. Whatever operator sequence both bases agree on for Spider or Nitro Mirror — that's signal. Whatever diverges is basis-artifact, not corpus-artifact.

That also reframes the earlier ghost-knot/Δ correspondence I flagged — it holds regardless of which basis you use to name it, which is a decent argument that it's closer to invariant than to rendering. Worth keeping; the specific letters aren't.